From 9fe1e4df901bfe92631160d05f173fa2fef89002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:42:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Adapt pinned Guix builds to headless Lotus with independent
 Chronik and NNG

Use the validated Rust 1.93.0 toolchain and current Lotus CMake target and option names. Retain built-in wallet support with BDB and SQLite, while omitting Qt, ZeroMQ and unrelated auxiliary tools. Build and run C++ checks before security and symbol checks; package the binaries with upstream COPYING. Repair the inherited missing CPython endif and pass the depends toolchain explicitly to existing NNG and Flatbuffers recipes. Fetch the unchanged pinned Guix revision from its canonical Codeberg repository after the obsolete Savannah URL failed in CI. Initial validation is limited to x86_64-linux-gnu; no macOS ARM cross-build claim is made.

Actual Guix CI 38613625044 reached dependency compilation with GCC 14.3 and exposed FlatBuffers 2.0.0 assigning to a const span count. Backport exactly upstream google/flatbuffers 20aad0c41e1252b04c72111c3eb221280a9c2009 for both native flatc and target headers, retaining the pinned archive hash and protocol version. Share that original mailbox through the native patch-directory symlink. This build-only repair does not change NNG schemas or consensus.

The next Guix attempt built every depends package but Lotus CMake could not find Jemalloc: the Lotus Linux64 toolchain reads the CMake BASEPREFIX variable rather than its environment. Pass that prefix explicitly to the main configuration as well as package builds, preserving upstream dependency discovery and static linking.

A parallel GNU14 native depends probe reached C++ test compilation and exposed Boost1.81 redefining numeric_limits<__float128>. Backport original Boost.Math f51e1b985de4d739a9f40d8c2b90debd419da267 and b3a21bcbcbd95c23da594ff53c3a48e38f0ff9f3 mailboxes, preserving archive/version and using the final libstdc++ release guard. This is compiler compatibility, not removal of the intmath tests or replacement of mining code.

Actual Guix run 38643046586 exposed a leak from this adaptation: exporting CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE cross-configured native code-generator child builds. Run 38658338757 then showed that clearing the export only after depends was insufficient: native protoc and flatc had already been cross-built with the glibc 2.31 loader, and failed when Cargo's native library environment selected glibc requiring GLIBC_2.35. Never export the target toolchain; pass it explicitly only to target dependency recipes and main CMake. Check both native generator versions in the real Cargo library environment before compilation. Native GNU14 workflow step boundaries and its single libc baseline had hidden both differences.
